      "id": 1,
      "text": "Rosedale Park was originally deeded in 1835 to Otis C., setting the stage for Detroit's first suburban development.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosedale_Park%2C_Detroit"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1916, the Rosedale Park Land Development Company platted the area that would become the historic district.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosedale_Park%2C_Detroit"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The district earned a spot on the National Register of Historic Places in 2006, honoring its architectural heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosedale_Park%2C_Detroit"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Oak and maple trees, many planted in the 1910s and 1920s, line the neighborhood's tree‑lined streets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosedale_Park%2C_Detroit"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Only 15 houses appeared between 1917 and World War I, but construction surged by 1921 with a third addition.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
